                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Ok guys, I don't precisely know what a T-Kara is. And it's not explained in-game..\nBut it's definitely a nice casual match-4 one!\nYeah, be aware that all the puzzles - and there will be tons of them - are all based on a match-4 (or more) tiles of the same color, so mechanisms are quite different to the usual match-3 we're all used to.\nThis particular game features a minimalistic and simple interface that puts the user towards many different kinds of puzzles, which all share in common the feature of being set in the same big structure with the same colored blocks, although you'll have different goals for every single kind of puzzle you choose.\nYou'll be able to play normal match-4 games, match-4 grids in which you're forced to make all the matches in a particular section of the board, games in which some tiles will be blocked and you'll have to put the fixed tile within a combination of 4 or more pieces, or even levels with the left bar, that usually serves as the progression bar to reach your goal, will be filled with colors pointing you towards the particular tiles you'll have to match.\nOf course, all the levels will have limited moves and three stars per level, meaning that if you want the three of them you'll have to solve them within the least moves you'll be able to employ.\nThere are six different kinds of match-4 puzzles and there are to be more precise 48 levels for every one of them, meaning that there's tons of levels and this game is super for what concerns the casual tag since you can for instance play a single level for the six (or less, whatever you want, the progress is always automatically saved) types of puzzle and take the game on after a day or two. Or two weeks, like me, during this review.\nPlus, the game features also a score attack/survival mode which will be essentially a timed/limited version of the normal match-4 puzzles (I sincerely recommend you to play the normal campaign for at least a dozen of normal levels in order to get used to the game mechanisms - even if they're simple); you'll be able to choose to have always limited moves, or simply a timer; the third survival campaign revolves around a direct hit format which will be essentially an endless mode with bonuses given by 5+ combinations and so on.\nThere's also a relaxing picture puzzle mode, in which you'll be asked for dozens of pictures all revolving towards a \"space/universe\" theme (which is the theme that links the whole game) to use the normal match-4 mechanisms but this time in order to recreate a picture, which will be scattered through the tiles in the board.\nAnd if this wasn't enough, there's also a daily challenge section (much like the Android mobile games xD) and a multiplayer one! With Steam turn notifications :)\nOh well but I'm a single player and I'm not here to talk about multiplayer - there's the chance though and it seems quite well done if you've got a friend to play with (don't count on random games).\nI think that for being an indie game this one is worthy all the money it costs. It's addicting and even if it's clear that it's an indie title given its graphics and such, it's loaded with literally hundreds of different and addicting puzzles. There are no bugs at all and you can play it casually from time to time, whenever you want.\nIf you like match-3 games and you want to have a new experience with a nice, relaxing and also challenging match-4 game this one's for sure suited for you, and if you love puzzle/logic games I'd give a try to this awesome game :)\nDefinitely recommended for every puzzle/logic game lover!",
